<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cassius Longinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 213–273</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Dionysius Cassius Longinus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cassius Longinus was a Greek rhetorician and Platonist philosopher who taught for many years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where Porphyry was among his pupils. He later joined the court of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> as her adviser. After the Roman emperor Aurelian captured Palmyra in 272, Longinus was executed on the charge of having encouraged Zenobia's revolt. He was long credited with the treatise On the Sublime, though most scholars now doubt the attribution.</p></div>
